<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco de Montejo the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1508–<a href="/years/1565">1565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco de Montejo the Younger was a Spanish conquistador, son of the adelantado <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Francisco de Montejo</a>. After his father's failed campaigns he led the decisive conquest of northern Yucatán, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> in 1540 and Mérida in 1542 on the site of the Maya city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tho">T'ho</a>. Lords such as the Pech submitted, while the eastern provinces were subdued after a great Maya revolt in 1546–1547.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger.jpg" alt="Francisco de Montejo the Younger" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yodigo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monumento_a_los_Montejo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francisco de Montejo y León, called el Mozo, the Younger, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Francisco de Montejo</a>. Around June 1527 he sailed with his father and his cousin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-nephew">Francisco de Montejo the Nephew</a>, from Sanlúcar de Barrameda to Cozumel, the voyage that opened the first military campaign of the conquest of Yucatán.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1528 he went to Santa María de la Victoria, the first Spanish town in Mexican territory, founded at the mouth of the San Pedro River in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>, with the task of pacifying the region. When his father left for Yucatán in 1530 he took over the campaign. He had subdued almost the whole region of the Grijalva River when the First Court dismissed his father, then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, and named Baltazar Osorio mayor of Tabasco, forcing el Mozo to leave Santa María de la Victoria and await his father's orders.</p><p>After an indigenous uprising against the Spanish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> in 1535, the Second Court restored the elder Montejo, who sent his son back to pacify the province, a task partly achieved by 1537. In 1539 el Mozo was made captain general and governor of Tabasco, but in 1540 he left to join his father's new attempt on Yucatán. In 1542 he obtained the surrender of the western part of the peninsula and founded Mérida on the former Maya city of Ichkansihóo, or T'Hó, which was then almost abandoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the conquest he lived on in Mérida, which later became the capital of the Mexican state of Yucatán. In his later years he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, where he died after a long illness on 8 February 1565.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco de Montejo the Younger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Francisco de Montejo the Younger</b> made the city his capital, Ciudad Real, but the Maya besieged his force and he abandoned it in 1534.</blockquote><a href="/work/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1541 the Canul lords submitted peacefully to <b>Francisco de Montejo the Younger</b>, and the province was absorbed into the Province of Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/state/ah-canul">Ah Canul</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pech chose to ally with <b>Francisco de Montejo the Younger</b> in 1541, and one of its nobles, Nakuk Pech, later wrote the Chronicle of Chicxulub, a Maya account of the conquest.</blockquote><a href="/state/ceh-pech">Ceh Pech</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1542 <b>Francisco de Montejo the Younger</b> founded Mérida on the site of T'ho, using its stones for the new buildings, and Chakan became the core of the Province of Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/state/chakan">Chakan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1537 <b>Francisco de Montejo the Younger</b> established a Spanish base at Champotón, from which the conquest of Yucatán was launched.</blockquote><a href="/state/champoton">Champotón</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1542 Ah Kukum Xiu came to T'ho and submitted to <b>Montejo the Younger</b>, giving the Spanish a vital ally.</blockquote><a href="/state/mani">Maní</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.39,-6&amp;year=1545" aria-label="Seville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4400 818 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francisco de Montejo the Younger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4850 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4850 1088h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.39,-6&amp;year=1545">What was here in 1545? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
